{"id":"pattern-example-001","type":"pattern","fact":"Example: Use mock factories for all test data instead of manual mock objects with 'as Type' assertions.","recommendation":"Create centralized mock factory functions (createMockUser, createMockOrg, etc.) and import them in tests.","confidence":"high","provenance":[{"source":"documentation","reference":"Testing guide","date":"2026-01-09"}],"tags":["testing","mocks","factories"],"affectedFiles":["**/*.test.ts"],"affectedServices":[],"createdAt":"2026-01-09T00:00:00Z","updatedAt":"2026-01-09T00:00:00Z","usageCount":0,"helpfulCount":0,"outdatedReports":0}
{"id":"pattern-bats-hermetic-mocks","type":"pattern","fact":"The bats suite runs hermetic - no Docker, no IONOS credentials. test_helper.bash copies the CLI into a per-test temp dir and prepends mock 'docker'/'pass' to PATH.","recommendation":"Any new test reaching a Docker or credential path must use mock_docker_running/mock_docker_stopped/mock_pass_empty from test/test_helper.bash; never call real Docker.","confidence":"high","provenance":[{"source":"agent","reference":"test harness, issue #7","date":"2026-05-18"}],"tags":["testing","bats","mocks","docker"],"affectedFiles":["test/*.bats","test/test_helper.bash"],"affectedServices":[],"createdAt":"2026-05-18T00:00:00Z","updatedAt":"2026-05-18T00:00:00Z","usageCount":0,"helpfulCount":0,"outdatedReports":0}
{"id": "pattern-autorenew-deploy-hook-plus-timer", "type": "pattern", "fact": "Auto-renewal is two decoupled pieces. (1) Reload: a certbot deploy hook at $EASY_LETSENCRYPT_DIR/renewal-hooks/deploy/reload-nginx.sh containing 'nginx -c /usr/local/share/easy/nginx.conf -s reload'. It lives on the host volume so it survives destroy && create, certbot executes it INSIDE the container so it must use container paths, and it fires only when a certificate actually renewed - never on a no-op run. Without the executable bit certbot skips it silently, with no error. 'certbot renew --dry-run' does exercise it, so it is testable. (2) Schedule: a systemd timer running 'docker exec easy-proxy certbot renew --quiet'. Do not add a separate reload job - the hook covers it. Once a lineage exists, 'certbot renew' is the right command; re-running 'easy proxy certbot-ionos' forces a fresh issuance and burns rate limit.", "recommendation": "Schedule twice daily (OnCalendar=*-*-* 03,15:00:00, RandomizedDelaySec=3600, Persistent=true), never monthly: renew is a no-op above 30 days from expiry so frequency is free, while a single monthly attempt leaves no recovery margin if it fails. Verify the whole chain with 'certbot renew --dry-run' plus 'systemctl list-timers' (a date in NEXT) and a manual 'systemctl start' of the oneshot unit (expect status=0/SUCCESS and 'Cert not yet due for renewal'). Export EASY_LETSENCRYPT_DIR before creating the hook directory - an unset variable silently creates /renewal-hooks in the host filesystem root where certbot will never look.", "confidence": "high", "provenance": [{"source": "agent", "reference": "production session production: first working IONOS DNS-01 integration + auto-renewal", "date": "2026-09-09"}], "tags": ["certbot", "letsencrypt", "systemd", "renewal", "deploy-hook", "nginx"], "affectedFiles": ["commands/proxy.sh", "SERVER_RUNBOOK.md"], "affectedServices": [], "createdAt": "2026-09-09T00:00:00Z", "updatedAt": "2026-09-09T00:00:00Z", "usageCount": 0, "helpfulCount": 0, "outdatedReports": 0}
{"id": "pattern-switch-lineage-to-http01-webroot", "type": "pattern", "fact": "A lineage stuck on 'authenticator = manual' (e.g. a wildcard on a DNS provider without a certbot plugin) can be converted in place to HTTP-01 webroot by re-running certonly with the SAME --cert-name. certbot rewrites renewal/<name>.conf (authenticator = webroot, webroot_map) and keeps live/<name>/ paths, so existing vhosts need no change and no <name>-0001 lineage appears. Prerequisite: a port-80 vhost serving 'location ^~ /.well-known/acme-challenge/ { root /var/www/certbot; }' with everything else redirected to HTTPS.", "recommendation": "Order: cp the old renewal conf to renewal.disabled/ as a record; write the port-80 vhost; probe the path with a file in /var/www/certbot/.well-known/acme-challenge/ and curl it via public DNS; then 'docker exec easy-proxy certbot certonly --non-interactive --agree-tos --webroot -w /var/www/certbot --cert-name <d> -d <d> -d www.<d> --dry-run', then without --dry-run; finally 'certbot renew --dry-run' must exit 0 for ALL lineages.", "confidence": "high", "provenance": [{"source": "agent", "reference": "production operations", "date": "2026-09-10"}], "tags": ["letsencrypt", "http-01", "webroot", "certbot", "renewal"], "affectedFiles": ["easyhome/templates/http.conf", "Dockerfile"], "affectedServices": [], "createdAt": "2026-09-10T00:00:00Z", "updatedAt": "2026-09-10T00:00:00Z", "usageCount": 0, "helpfulCount": 0, "outdatedReports": 0}
{"id": "pattern-domains-dir-under-git", "type": "pattern", "fact": "EASY_DOMAINS_DIR (bind-mounted as /domains) holds only nginx vhosts - no secrets - so it is safe and cheap to keep under git, locally and on a private remote. nginx only includes /domains/*/*.conf, so a top-level README.md is not loaded.", "recommendation": "Commit the state as found before the first change. Then every vhost change: edit -> 'docker exec easy-proxy nginx -c /usr/local/share/easy/nginx.conf -t' -> reload -> curl checks (including the other sites) -> git commit with the why. Prefer git history over ad-hoc copies (domains.bak-<date>) for rollback.", "confidence": "high", "provenance": [{"source": "agent", "reference": "production operations", "date": "2026-09-10"}], "tags": ["nginx", "ops", "git", "production"], "affectedFiles": [], "affectedServices": [], "createdAt": "2026-09-10T00:00:00Z", "updatedAt": "2026-09-10T00:00:00Z", "usageCount": 0, "helpfulCount": 0, "outdatedReports": 0}
